---
# POSTERS: is every card still drawing a poster, from outside and from inside.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. On 2026-10-21 every kinoheld poster in Germany rendered as "no poster" --
# 1,443 film pages whose primary poster is kinoheld's, 762 more where it was the fallback -- and
# nothing alerted (5a77a8867). PosterProxy handed weserv the origin scheme-less, weserv fetched it
# over http, static.kinoheld.de answers http with 403, and weserv returned its own 404. The page
# answered 200, the pods were green, every web and worker counter was clean: the image is fetched by
# the visitor's browser from images.weserv.nl, so no request of ours ever failed. Earlier poster
# breakages had the same silence -- Multikino and Amazon refusing weserv (2026-05/07), AlloCine
# blocked by weserv's edge (2026-07-19), mixed-content http posters (2026-05-21) -- each found by a
# human looking at the site.
#
# THREE LAYERS, each seeing what the one before cannot:
#
#   delivery  PosterProbeFailing / PosterProbesMissing. blackbox_exporter on monitoring-1 fetches,
#             every five minutes, two posters per poster host per country exactly as a city page
#             hands them to a browser (synthetic-probe-targets.sh: weserv URL included, so a fault
#             in how the URL is BUILT is probed as much as the host), and asks for an image over
#             https. The only layer that sees an image fail to load -- and the one that catches
#             2026-10-21: both kinoheld probes would have read weserv's `404 application/json`.
#   coverage  PosterHostLostFromServing / PosterPlaceholderShareJumped. The web's own count of the
#             films each country serves by whose poster their card draws (`kinowo_web_films_by_
#             poster_host`, WebMovieMetrics.PosterCensus) -- `none` being the "no poster"
#             placeholder. A scraper that stops extracting a chain's posters, a projection change
#             that drops a source, a filter that throws good posters away: no image fails, the
#             cards just stop asking for one.
#   identity  PosterSharedImageJump. Films whose poster is ONE image three or more films share --
#             a site-wide og:image (Kinoteka, 2026-10-06), a "coming soon" card (Multikino
#             `wkrotce`, 2026-06-18), a not-found image (bilety24, 2026-09-24), a placeholder
#             (Flicks, 2026-10-01). Each loads fine and is the wrong picture.
#
# NOT ON THE BROWSER BEACON. /uptime/img-event's per-host rows (`kinowo_uptime_recent_*{service=~
# "img: .*"}`) are charted in the Posters row, not alerted: measured over 2026-10-07..21, a
# country's real-user primary loads number 0-10 per half hour, and their failure share swings
# 0..100% on a healthy day (uk 1.0 twice, pl 0.8 twice). There is no threshold in that.
#
# THRESHOLDS. Two comparisons are against the same hour yesterday: a country's served film count
# moves by up to 21% day on day on its own (kinowo_web_movies_served, its biggest city, max over
# 2026-10-07..21: us 21%, de 20%, es 20%, pl 4%, uk 3%), so a poster host's SHARE halving (not its
# count) and the placeholder share moving ten points are well clear of that churn. Not backtested:
# the gauges and probes are new with this file -- `infra/bin/backtest-alerts` against the first
# fortnight is the check.
#
# RESTART-GAP-SAFE: the web gauges and the probe series are read through `last_over_time`, so a
# web rollout (~minutes without a sample) does not reset a `for:` (test_alert_rule_coverage.py).
# The names all start `Poster` so alertmanager.yaml's email route enrols them by prefix.
groups:
  - name: posters
    rules:
      # THE WEB'S POSTER CENSUS, ONE SERIES PER (country, poster_host), and each host's share of
      # the country's served films. `max` collapses replicas of one country's web tier, which all
      # serve the same read model.
      - record: country_poster_host:web_films:max
        expr: max by (country, poster_host) (last_over_time(kinowo_web_films_by_poster_host[10m]))
      - record: country_poster_host:web_films:share
        expr: >-
          country_poster_host:web_films:max
            / on (country) group_left
          sum by (country) (country_poster_host:web_films:max)
      - record: country:web_films_sharing_poster:sum
        expr: >-
          sum by (country) (max by (country, poster_host) (last_over_time(kinowo_web_films_sharing_poster[10m])))

      # EVERY POSTER PROBED FOR A HOST HAS FAILED FOR TEN MINUTES. Two posters per host, probed
      # every five minutes, so this is both of them failing three probes running; `max` over the
      # host's probes is what lets one dead image (a cinema that never uploaded the poster it lists)
      # stay quiet. A probe that answers a "successful" image under 1 KB counts as failed: weserv
      # and the CDNs answer a refused image with a tiny placeholder body as often as with an error.
      # Held while the probes' own path out is failing (ProbeSourceDegraded, synthetic-probes.rules).
      # Read over 11 minutes, so the hourly change of the probes' cache key (a new `instance`) and
      # a late scrape both stay bridged.
      - alert: PosterProbeFailing
        expr: >-
          max by (country, poster_host) (
            last_over_time(probe_success{kind="poster"}[11m])
              * (last_over_time(probe_http_uncompressed_body_length{kind="poster"}[11m]) >= bool 1024)
          ) == 0
          unless on () probe:control_failing:any
        for: 10m
        labels:
          severity: critical
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: every {{ $labels.poster_host }} poster probed from outside fails to load"
          description: >-
            blackbox_exporter on monitoring-1 cannot load any of the {{ $labels.poster_host }}
            posters it took from {{ $labels.country }}'s city page, as a browser is handed them,
            for ten minutes -- so cards drawing that host's poster are showing "no poster" (or
            their fallback) to every visitor. `probe_http_status_code{kind="poster",
            poster_host="{{ $labels.poster_host }}"}` and the `instance` URL say which kind: a weserv
            URL answering 404 is weserv unable to fetch the origin -- curl the origin yourself, with
            and without https (2026-10-21: the scheme was stripped, kinoheld 403s plain http); one
            answering 400 is a URL PosterProxy built wrong; a direct CDN URL failing is the CDN.
            A 200 that still failed is not an image (`Content-Type`) or under 1 KB, or an http://
            poster a browser refuses on an https page.

      # THE COUNTRY'S PAGES LIST FILMS BUT NOT ONE PROBEABLE POSTER. The discovery found the
      # country's film (so the city page answered and lists films) and no poster host drawing ten
      # or more of its posters: the page's cards have stopped carrying posters at all -- or every
      # one went to a skipped host. Either way PosterProbeFailing cannot fire for this country.
      - alert: PosterProbesMissing
        expr: >-
          count by (country) (last_over_time(probe_success{kind="film"}[15m]))
            unless
          count by (country) (last_over_time(probe_success{kind="poster"}[15m]))
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}'s city page names no poster to probe"
          description: >-
            The synthetic probes found a film on {{ $labels.country }}'s city page but no poster host
            drawing ten or more of its cards' posters (synthetic-probe-targets.sh), so no poster is
            being watched from outside. Look at the page: if the cards show "no poster", the posters
            went missing before the browser (PosterPlaceholderShareJumped should agree); if they
            show posters, they all come from hosts in `fleet.syntheticProbes.posterSkipHosts` or
            under the floor -- `journalctl -u synthetic-probe-targets` and the page's
            `data-original-src` attributes say which.

      # A POSTER HOST'S SHARE OF THE COUNTRY'S FILMS HALVED SINCE YESTERDAY, for a host that drew at
      # least fifty films then. A host that VANISHED (its series gone, or folded under `other`
      # below the web's floor) reads as a share of zero through `or 0 * …offset`, which is the
      # case this is for: a scraper that stopped extracting a chain's posters takes the host to
      # nothing. Held half an hour so a read-model rebuild's minute does not fire it.
      - alert: PosterHostLostFromServing
        expr: >-
          (
            (
              country_poster_host:web_films:share{poster_host!~"none|other"}
                or 0 * country_poster_host:web_films:share{poster_host!~"none|other"} offset 1d
            )
              < 0.5 * country_poster_host:web_films:share{poster_host!~"none|other"} offset 1d
          )
          and on (country, poster_host) country_poster_host:web_films:max offset 1d >= 50
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: films drawing a {{ $labels.poster_host }} poster fell to {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of the country's, from over twice that yesterday"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_web_films_by_poster_host{country="{{ $labels.country }}"}` -- the films the web
            serves, by whose poster their card draws -- has {{ $labels.poster_host }}'s share under
            half of what it was 24 hours ago. The posters have not failed to load (that is
            PosterProbeFailing); the cards stopped asking for them. Where did the films go: a rise
            in `poster_host="none"` is posters lost outright (a scraper no longer extracting them,
            or a filter now rejecting them -- PlaceholderPoster, CinemaFallbackPosters); a rise in
            another host is the projection preferring a different source. `git log` on the chain's
            scraper and on MovieRecord.posterUrl's ordering.

      # THE PLACEHOLDER SHARE ROSE TEN POINTS SINCE YESTERDAY: one in ten of the country's served
      # films more is drawn with "no poster" than a day ago. Normal levels differ by country (de
      # ~20%, uk ~9%, pl ~1% on 2026-10-21 city pages), so this watches the move, not the level.
      - alert: PosterPlaceholderShareJumped
        expr: >-
          country_poster_host:web_films:share{poster_host="none"}
            - country_poster_host:web_films:share{poster_host="none"} offset 1d
          > 0.10
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} more of the served films have no poster than yesterday"
          description: >-
            The share of {{ $labels.country }}'s served films whose card renders the "no poster"
            placeholder (`kinowo_web_films_by_poster_host{poster_host="none"}`) rose by over ten
            points in 24 hours. PosterHostLostFromServing names the host the posters were lost from,
            if one host accounts for it; if none does, a change to the poster ordering or filtering
            (MovieRecord.posterUrl, PlaceholderPoster) is dropping them across sources.

      # A BURST OF FILMS SHARING ONE IMAGE: twenty-five more films than yesterday, and half as many
      # again, now draw a poster three or more films share. Some sharing is normal (2026-10-21:
      # kinoheld's `fallback-light.jpg` on 23 Berlin films, Nove Kino's `plakat.jpg` on 18 in
      # Warsaw), so this watches the jump, not the level.
      - alert: PosterSharedImageJump
        expr: >-
          country:web_films_sharing_poster:sum >= 25 + country:web_films_sharing_poster:sum offset 1d
          and
          country:web_films_sharing_poster:sum >= 1.5 * country:web_films_sharing_poster:sum offset 1d
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }}: {{ $value }} served films now draw a poster image three or more films share"
          description: >-
            `kinowo_web_films_sharing_poster` -- films whose poster is one image shared with two or
            more others -- jumped since yesterday. That is a placeholder, a site-wide og:image or a
            "coming soon" card taken for a poster: it loads fine and is the wrong picture. The
            `poster_host` label on the series says whose; the city page's `data-original-src`
            attributes name the image. Past cases: Kinoteka's site-wide og:image (1101ebc6e),
            Multikino's `wkrotce` card (b8e17077e), bilety24's not-found image (9aa94c8c4).
